# Don't Build on the Approach: Why This Data Center Fails the Test That Matters

At 6 a.m. a United widebody breaks out of the clouds on final to Runway 19R at Dulles, a few hundred feet over a patch of ground the airport now proposes to sell to a data-center developer. That patch is not ordinary land. It sits inside the approach and departure surfaces of a precision runway, in the least-forgiving phase of flight, on real estate the airport's own board is spending decades trying to protect. And the proposal to pave it arrived not as a master-plan amendment but as a forwarded project email.

MWAA should not build this data center as sited. Not because any single hazard is disqualifying — none is — but because the siting forecloses irreversible airfield capacity, and because the decision is being made through the wrong door.

Start with the mistake most reviewers make. They read the objection as a hazard list — glare, thermal plume, electromagnetic interference, wildlife — and then discover, correctly, that every item on that list is an *evaluable, conditionable* FAA question. The FAA rates the overall flight-disruption risk from thermal plumes as low, though the agency notes plumes may pose a unique hazard to aircraft in critical phases of flight — takeoff, landing, and within the pattern.[^1] Solar glare has a screening policy. Navigational interference has an evaluation path. Run the list item by item and the project survives, wrapped in conditions. That is why a hazard-by-hazard fight is a fight the objection loses.

The argument that does not dissolve under FAA process is about the land itself.

Runway 1L/19R is 9,400 feet of precision-instrument concrete, opened in 2008, with high-intensity approach lighting at both ends.[^2] Under 14 CFR 77.19 its approach surface rises at just 50 to 1.[^3] Do the arithmetic that geometry forces: a 50-foot object reaches that surface only about 2,500 feet beyond the runway end.[^4] Data-hall roofs, rooftop chillers, and exhaust stacks can easily clear 50 feet, so "it doesn't penetrate" is a razor-thin margin, not a comfortable clearance. The departure surface is tighter still — 40 to 1, which permits only 25 feet of object height 1,000 feet off the departure end.[^5] And the aircraft that overflies this parcel is low: roughly 100 feet above the ground at 1,000 feet from the threshold, about 200 feet at 3,000 feet.[^6] Whatever a plume or a glare source does, it does it exactly where the pilot has the least room to recover. The proposed stormwater pond compounds the problem: FAA guidance asks for 10,000 feet of separation between a wildlife attractant and turbine operations, and a pond at the runway end sits roughly 95 percent inside that standard — even on a conservative reading of the site geometry.[^7]

Now the decisive point. This is board-affirmed capacity land. On July 16, 2025, the MWAA Board adopted an updated master plan that carries Dulles toward roughly 90 million annual passengers and reserves airfield expansion for the decades ahead.[^8] You cannot un-pour a data center. Commit this ground to a 30-year lease and a slab, and the airport has traded a permanent constraint on its runway environment for a term of rent — a decision no future capacity forecast can reverse.

Frame the objection the way the law now frames it. Section 163 of the 2018 FAA Reauthorization Act deliberately narrowed federal authority over on-airport land use to development that (A) materially impacts safe and efficient aircraft operation, (B) adversely affects safety of people or property on the ground, or (C) adversely affects the value of prior federal investment.[^9] That test looks like a limit on the FAA. Here it is the airport's strongest instrument, because the parcel implicates all three prongs — the operating surfaces of an active runway, the ground under a low approach, and decades of federal investment in that runway and its navigational aids. The airport's grant obligations — Assurances 19, 20, 21, 22, and 29 — bind it to the same standard: keep the layout plan current, keep terminal airspace protected, keep nearby land compatible with normal operations.[^10]

The counter-case is real, and it deserves a straight answer. Airports are told to be as self-sustaining as possible, and non-aeronautical land near power and fiber is among the most valuable acreage in American aviation. MWAA has done exactly this deal before: in 2018 it sold 424 acres of Dulles "Western Lands" to Digital Realty for $236.5 million, proceeds directed explicitly to hold down the cost airlines pay per passenger.[^11] If that was compliant and prudent then, why is this reckless now?

Because the 2018 sale is the rule this proposal breaks, not the precedent that excuses it. The Western Lands were the surplus that *remained after the fourth runway and its support area were built.* MWAA protected capacity first and monetized what was left over. The discipline was sequence: reserve, then sell. This proposal inverts it — monetize first, inside the surfaces of a runway the airport is actively planning to keep and extend. That is not the same play; it is the opposite one.

The rest of the counter-case concedes rather than refutes. Yes, the overall plume risk is low. Yes, the hazards are conditionable. Grant all of it. Conditions can cure a plume. Conditions cannot give back foreclosed land, and they cannot substitute for the review this project skipped.

So the recommendation is a gate, not a wall. An airport of this consequence does not approve a runway-adjacent land conversion off a forwarded email. Before this proposal earns a hearing, it needs FAA district-office coordination, a Part 77 aeronautical study run against the real site plan, glare, plume, and interference analyses, a stormwater design that respects wildlife separation, and a formal layout-plan amendment. And even if every study returns "no hazard," this siting should still be rejected — because the question was never whether the building is safe. The question is whether this ground is the airport's to give away. It is not yet, and it may never be.

Other working airports have already reached this conclusion. In July 2026, Inyokern Airport and NAWS China Lake formally opposed a hyperscale data center near their field, citing thermal plume from 40 diesel generators as a threat to navigable airspace.[^12] They read the runway environment as something to defend. Dulles should read its own the same way — before the concrete sets the answer for it.

[^1]: FAA, "Thermal Exhaust Plume Impact on Airport Operations: Technical Guidance and Assessment Tool" (based on MITRE Exhaust-Plume-Analyzer), which finds overall flight-disruption risk is low but notes plumes may pose a unique hazard to aircraft in critical phases of flight — particularly takeoff, landing, and within the pattern.

[^2]: Runway 01L/19R dimensions (9,400 ft × 150 ft) per FlightAware airport record, KIAD; service began November 2008; both runway ends equipped with high-intensity (ALSF-2) approach lighting per FAA approach-plate records.

[^3]: 14 CFR § 77.19, Civil Airport Imaginary Surfaces — precision-instrument approach surface rising at 50:1 for the first 10,000 ft.

[^4]: Analyst calculation from the § 77.19 50:1 slope: maximum object height = distance from surface origin ÷ 50; a 50-ft object reaches the surface at ~2,500 ft, a 70-ft object at ~3,500 ft. Assumes flat terrain at runway-end elevation pending the operator's surveyed site plan.

[^5]: FAA AC 150/5300-13B, Airport Design — departure obstacle clearance surface slopes at 40:1 from the departure end of runway; 40:1 permits only 25 ft of object height at 1,000 ft off the departure end.

[^6]: Analyst calculation from standard 3-degree glidepath geometry (50 ft threshold crossing height): overflight height ≈ 102 ft AGL at 1,000 ft from threshold, ~207 ft at 3,000 ft.

[^7]: FAA AC 150/5200-33C, Hazardous Wildlife Attractants on or Near Airports, recommends 10,000 ft separation between a hazardous wildlife attractant and the aircraft operations area for airports serving turbine-powered aircraft. The ~95% figure is an analyst estimate based on the physical proximity of a runway-end pond to the threshold; actual shortfall depends on the surveyed site plan.

[^8]: Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority, "Dulles Master Plan Update Sets Framework for Decades of Airport Development," adopted by the MWAA Board July 16, 2025 (build-out toward ~90 million annual passengers, with reserved airfield expansion).

[^9]: Section 163, FAA Reauthorization Act of 2018 (as revised by Section 743 of the FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024), limiting FAA authority over on-airport land use to development meeting the (A)/(B)/(C) test.

[^10]: FAA Airport Sponsor Assurances (AIP) / Order 5190.6C, Assurances 19 (Operation & Maintenance), 20 (Hazard Removal & Mitigation), 21 (Compatible Land Use), 22 (Economic Nondiscrimination), and 29 (Airport Layout Plan).

[^11]: Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority, "The Airports Authority Announces Sale of 424 Acres of Western Lands at Dulles International" — $236.5 million to Digital Realty, proceeds directed to hold down future cost per enplaned passenger.

[^12]: "Inyokern Airport joins opposition to proposed data center, citing community impacts," BakersfieldNow, July 8, 2026 — Inyokern Airport and NAWS China Lake opposing a data center over thermal plume from 40 diesel generators and impacts to navigable airspace. A live objection, not an adjudicated FAA ruling.
